# Architecture

> **Note:** The stack was fully replaced on 2026-05-04. Previous references to PowerSync, Supabase, `stride-desktop` as a React app, or `@stridetime/db`/`@stridetime/sync` as a PowerSync package are obsolete. See `decisions.mdc` 2026-05-04 entry for full context.

## Monorepo Structure

```
apps/
  web/          TanStack Start — SSR build for web (CF Workers), SPA build for Tauri
  desktop/      Tauri 2.0 shell ONLY — Rust, tauri.conf.json, native plugins. No React code here.
  mobile/       Expo / React Native (future)
  api/          Hono on Cloudflare Workers — all business logic
packages/
  ui/           Shared React components — no business logic, no API calls
  api-client/   Typed Hono hc client — used by web, desktop (SPA), and mobile
  queue/        Mutation queue abstraction — pure TypeScript, storage driver injected per runtime
  db/           Drizzle schema + drizzle-zod inferred types — imported by api/ only
```

## Dependency Rules

Each layer may only import from layers listed. Never import upward or sideways outside these rules.

| Package | May import from |
|---|---|
| `packages/db` | nothing (leaf — Drizzle schema + zod types only) |
| `packages/ui` | nothing (leaf — React components, no data fetching) |
| `packages/api-client` | `packages/db` (for shared types) |
| `packages/queue` | nothing (leaf — pure TS interfaces + platform-agnostic logic) |
| `apps/api` | `packages/db` |
| `apps/web` | `packages/ui`, `packages/api-client`, `packages/queue` |
| `apps/desktop` | nothing (Rust/Tauri config only — frontend is the web SPA build) |
| `apps/mobile` | `packages/ui`, `packages/api-client`, `packages/queue` |

`apps/web` **never** imports from `apps/api` directly — all server communication goes through `packages/api-client` (typed Hono `hc` client) or TanStack Start server functions (which call Hono route handlers in-process on CF Workers).

## Type Flow

```
Drizzle schema (packages/db)
  → drizzle-zod inferred types (packages/db)
    → Hono route types (apps/api)
      → hc typed client (packages/api-client)
        → component props (apps/web, apps/mobile)
```

Single source of truth from DB column to component prop. No manual type duplication.

## Build Targets — One Web Codebase, Two Outputs

`apps/web` is built two ways, controlled by `BUILD_TARGET` env var:

```bash
BUILD_TARGET=web     pnpm build  # SSR → deploys to CF Workers
BUILD_TARGET=desktop pnpm build  # SPA mode → dist/ → Tauri packages it
```

In `vite.config.ts`:
```ts
tanstackStart({
  spa: {
    enabled: process.env.BUILD_TARGET === 'desktop',
  },
})
```

**Data access goes through `packages/api-client`** (the typed Hono `hc` client), not TanStack Start server functions. The api-client's base URL is configurable (env var): `/api/$` on the same Worker for the web/SSR build; the remote API origin for the desktop SPA build — so the data layer is byte-identical across builds. Reserve TanStack Start server functions for things that genuinely need server-only context (request headers/cookies, secrets); they are not the data-fetching mechanism. *(Refines the 2026-05-04 `decisions.mdc` note about server functions in SPA mode — the api-client is the explicit, single data boundary.)*

## Tauri Desktop Architecture

One Tauri binary. Two windows. No separate React codebase — `apps/web`'s SPA build (`BUILD_TARGET=desktop`) **is** the desktop frontend.

- `apps/desktop/` is Rust + config only — `tauri.conf.json`, native plugins, the Rust backend. No React.
- `apps/desktop/tauri.conf.json` points `frontendDist` at `../web/dist/`.
- **Main window** — loads the SPA at `/`.
- **Tray window** — a second Tauri window, loads the same SPA at `/tray` (a TanStack Router route), smaller dimensions, anchored to the system tray.
- Both windows share **one native SQLite database** (Tauri SQL plugin) — the same `packages/queue` mutation-queue schema as the web build, with the native storage driver injected.
- The mutation-queue **drainer runs in the Rust backend**, not in a webview: single owner, survives window close; both webviews enqueue commands via Tauri commands. *(Refines the earlier "main window owns the drainer" framing — confirm when `apps/desktop` is built.)*
- The Hono API CORS allowlist includes the Tauri webview origin (`tauri://localhost` / `https://tauri.localhost`, per platform).

### Desktop specifics — planned approach

Refines the 2026-05-04 decision; to be ADR-logged and confirmed when `apps/desktop` is built (tracked in `docs/product/open-questions.md` Q18–Q20):

- **Auth transport: bearer token in the OS keychain**, not session cookies. Better Auth runs in cookie mode for web and bearer mode for desktop (it supports both). Sidesteps cross-site-cookie friction between the `tauri://` webview origin and the remote API.
- **OAuth callbacks** (connecting Jira / Linear / Google Calendar): `tauri-plugin-deep-link` (a custom protocol, e.g. `stride://oauth/callback`), or a localhost loopback the app spins up for the redirect. Decide when building the connect flows.
- **Offline reads**: the TanStack Query cache is persisted (`@tanstack/query-persist-client`) — OPFS on web, a file on desktop — so a cold start while offline shows last-fetched data, with the optimistic outbox layered over it.
- **Auto-update**: `tauri-plugin-updater` when it matters (not needed for the private dogfood v1).

## CF Workers Layout

```
CF Workers deployment
  ├── TanStack Start Worker   SSR + static assets (JS/CSS served directly, no R2 needed)
  │     └── /api/$            Hono catch-all — same Worker, zero network hop
  ├── Queue consumer Worker   Processes source sync events from CF Queue
  └── Cron Triggers           Jira polling fallback (webhooks are best-effort)
```

Hono is mounted inside TanStack Start as a catch-all API route. They share the same Worker process — no Worker-to-Worker network calls for API requests from the web app.

## Offline Architecture

Server-authoritative with optimistic updates and a durable SQLite mutation queue.

**Mutation flow:**
1. User action → serializable command with client UUID (idempotency key)
2. Optimistic update applied to TanStack Query cache immediately
3. Command written to SQLite queue (persists across app kills/reloads)
4. Background drainer sends commands to Hono API FIFO, exponential backoff retry
5. Server applies RLS, business rules, optimistic concurrency — returns canonical state
6. Server `processed_mutations` table enables safe idempotent retries
7. On conflict: 409 version conflict / 422 business rule / 403 permission → client rolls back optimistic state, surfaces rejected-mutation UI

**SQLite storage driver per runtime:**

| Runtime | Driver |
|---|---|
| Web | wa-sqlite over OPFS (requires COOP/COEP headers on CF Workers responses) |
| Desktop (Tauri) | Native SQLite via Tauri SQL plugin |
| Mobile (future) | expo-sqlite or op-sqlite |

`packages/queue` defines the interface. Each app injects its own driver. Queue logic is identical across all runtimes.

## Source Integration

**Sync boundaries:**

| Source | Stride Team maps to | Stride Project (optional) maps to |
|---|---|---|
| Jira (free/team-managed) | Space (one board per space) | Epic |
| Jira (paid/company-managed) | Board within a Space | Epic |
| Linear | Team | Linear Project |
| GitHub | Repository | Milestone |

**Rules:**
- All issues sync regardless of Epic/Project/Milestone membership — issues without groupings appear uncategorized, never hidden
- Status mapping is defined at connection time — admin maps source custom statuses to Stride internal states. UI always displays source vocabulary.
- Webhook ingest → CF Queue → consumer Worker → Neon. CF Cron Trigger polls Jira periodically (Jira webhooks are best-effort, no replay mechanism).
- Stride creates issues in source systems only during spec-splitting in refinement. Stride never creates Jira Spaces, Linear Teams, or GitHub Repos.

## Core Data Model

```
Spec          (synced from source — Jira/Linear/GitHub)
  └── Action  (Stride-native, 1+ per Spec)
        └── Session  (Stride-native, time block on an Action)

Standalone Action  (no parent Spec — lightweight personal task, title + estimate only)
  └── Session
```

**Rule: execution always ties to an Action. Sessions never attach directly to a Spec.**

A Spec always has at least one Action before work can begin. A 1:1 Spec→Action is valid for simple work.

Standalone Actions are personal, never appear in the team's shared spec backlog.

## Key Constraints

- **No Supabase.** Not used anywhere. Neon is the database.
- **No PowerSync.** Replaced by the mutation queue pattern above.
- **No TanStack DB yet.** TanStack Query only until TanStack DB persistence is stable (post v0.6).
- **No sessions on Specs.** Sessions always go through Actions.
- **No Stride-native Specs.** All Specs have a source system. Standalone tasks use Actions.
- **No separate Tauri React app.** `apps/desktop/` is Rust/config only. Web SPA build is the desktop frontend.
- **No separate tray binary.** Tray is a second window inside the same Tauri app.
